Notebook[{
Cell[TextData[StyleBox["INTRODUCTION:\nNumerical v.s. Symbolic\nComputing\n\n\
Monday, 7 July 2003",
  FontSize->24,
  FontWeight->"Bold"]], "Title"],

Cell["", "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Numerical Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "With some exceptions, most programming languages are designed for \
numerical computing, e.g., Fortran, C, and matlab package. The most obvious \
limitations are (1) limited range of integers, (2) finite accuracy of \
floating pointer numbers.",
  StyleBox["\n", "Subtitle"]
}], "Text"],

Cell["B. Symbolic Computing", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
Symbolic computing environment overcomes the two limitations (arbitrary sizes \
for integers, and a user-specified accuracy in floating point calculation).   \
Most importantly, symbolic packages provided ability of manipulating \
formulas, e.g., differentiation or integration of a function.  The \
disadvantage of symbolic systems is that it is typically many times slower \
than a compiled program.
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell["Symbolic Packages (Languages)", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
(1)  LISP:  low level but powerful programming language.
(2)  Maple, Mathematica:  two popular symbolic computing packages.
(3)  and many other special packages: reduce, axiom, macsyma, etc.
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
  FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]]], "Subtitle"],

Cell["A. Text-Based Interfaces", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Log into your UNIX system, type", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(math\)], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
You are engaged in a dialog with the system, one line at time, RETURN to \
enter an input.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["How to run Mathematica",
    FontColor->RGBColor[1, 0, 0]],
  "\nB. Notebook Interfaces (GUI)"
}], "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Log into your Window system (Start button, Programs, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  "),  click"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(mathematica\)], "Input"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "You can access the help as well as the whole book \"",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  ": a system for doing Mathematics by Computer\" by S. Wolfram."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[{
    RowBox[{\(IMPORTANT : \ You\ execute\ an\ Mathematica\ Expression\ with\),
       " "}], "\n", 
    RowBox[{"\t\t", 
      StyleBox[\(SHIFT - ENTER\),
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]]}]}], "Input"],

Cell["Arbitrary Large Integers", "Subtitle"],

Cell["Simple integer calculations", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2 + 2\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2^90\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1237940039285380274899124224\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    RowBox[{
      StyleBox["+",
        FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
      RowBox[{",", " ", 
        RowBox[{
          StyleBox["-",
            FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
          RowBox[{",", " ", 
            RowBox[{
              StyleBox["*",
                FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
              RowBox[{",", " ", 
                RowBox[{
                  StyleBox["/",
                    FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], 
                  RowBox[{",", " ", 
                    RowBox[{
                      RowBox[{
                        StyleBox["^",
                          FontColor->RGBColor[0, 0, 1]], " ", "\n", "for"}], 
                      " ", "add"}], ",", "subtract", ",", "multiply", ",", 
                    " ", "divide", ",", \(and\ power\), 
                    ",", \(as\ \(\(usual\)\(.\)\)\)}]}]}]}]}]}]}]}]], "Input"],

Cell["\<\
Integer functions (GCD[a,b], greatest common divisor, FactorInteger[a], etc):\
\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(GCD[10, 4]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(FactorInteger[40]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \({{2, 3}, {5, 1}}\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[TextData[{
  "This means 40 = ",
  Cell[BoxData[
      \(TraditionalForm\`2\^3\)]],
  " 5."
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    StyleBox[\(Function\ arguments\ are\ enclosed\ in\ square\ \(\(brakets\ [\
\  ... \ ]\)\(.\)\)\),
      FontColor->GrayLevel[0.500008]]], "Input"],

Cell["Floating Point with Arbitrary Precision", "Subtitle"],

Cell["\<\
The function N[...] converts an exact number into floating point. (N[ ] does \
not increase the precision of a number).\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(N[Pi, 25]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(3.141592653589793238462643383279502884`25\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
Mathematica's rules 

for numerical calculations:
    \t(1): If input is exact, the computation will be performed exactly.
    \t(2): If input is approximate, the result is approximate, with accuracy \
not great than the input.\
\>", "Theorem",
  FontSize->14,
  FontVariations->{"CompatibilityType"->0}],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[1.34]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(16\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Accuracy[134.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(14\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  = \ SetPrecision[1.3, \ 28]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1.300000000000000044408920985006261616945`28\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Precision[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(28\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(y\  = \ x\ *\ 1.0; \ Precision[y]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(16\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Why x has 28 digits of precision but y is only 16?", "Text"],

Cell["Symbol Data Type", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  "Like any programming languages, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provided with the usual data type of integer, floating point numbers and \
advanced structures.   In addition, ",
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " provides a type called \"Symbol\" for symbolic computation.  For \
example:"
}], "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(x\  + \ 3  x\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(4\ x\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\
The function Head[...] tells you what data type a particular expression is.\
\>", "Text"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Symbol\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integer\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[3\  + \ I\ ]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Complex\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Head[2.0]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Real\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["Some Examples of Symbolic Computation", "Subtitle"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Expand[\ \((1 + x)\)^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1 + 2\ x + x\^2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Factor[\ 1\  + \ 4\ x\  + \ 3  x^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\((1 + x)\)\ \((1 + 3\ x)\)\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Solve[\ {y^2\  + \ x\  - y\  == 0, \ 
        y\  - \ x^2\ \  + 2 \[Equal] \ 0}, \ {x, y}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[Sin[x], x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Cos[x]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(D[x^2, \ {x, \ 2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(2\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Integrate[\ \((1 + x)\)^3/\((1 - x^2)\)^2, \ x]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(\(-\(2\/\(\(-1\) + x\)\)\) + Log[\(-1\) + x]\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Simplify[Sin[x]^2\  + \ Cos[x]^2]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(1\)], "Output"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["\<\

Graphical Computations
\
\>", "Subtitle"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  " supports graphics in a convenient way, as well as more advanced \
programming environment.  A simple plot :"
}], "Text"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot[\ Sin[x], \ {x, \ \(-Pi\), \ Pi}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Plot3D[\ 
      Exp[\(-\((x^2 + y^2)\)\)], \ {x, \ \(-2\), 2}, \ {y, \ \(-2\), \ 
        2}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(g = \ 
      Graphics[\ {\ Circle[{1, 2}, 0.5], \ 
          Line[\ {{0, 0}, \ {1, 0}, \ {1, 1}}]}]\)], "Input"],

Cell[BoxData[
    \(Show[g, \ AspectRatio\  -> \ Automatic]\)], "Input"],

Cell["READINGS", "Title"],

Cell[TextData[{
  StyleBox["Introduction to ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSize->16,
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[":  section 1.1 to 1.6\nNotebook interface section 1.3.4 to 1.3.9.\
\n(Part of Chapter 1, A Practical Introduction to ",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSize->16,
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[").\n\nThe section number refers to the on-line \n",
    FontSize->16],
  StyleBox["Mathematica",
    FontSize->16,
    FontSlant->"Italic"],
  StyleBox[" book sections.",
    FontSize->16]
}], "Subtitle"]
},
FrontEndVersion->"4.1 for Microsoft Windows",
ScreenRectangle->{{0, 1024}, {0, 695}},
WindowSize->{1016, 668},
WindowMargins->{{0, Automatic}, {Automatic, 0}},
Magnification->3,
StyleDefinitions -> "Classroom.nb"
]

